A||||7||fiero56||my recommended cooling system fixes||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||08-05-2001||09:19 PM||||Just installed Rodney's low temp fan switch this morning. Now I never have to watch my temps in the drive-through lines again <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>My formula always ran HOT, and even with the fan on and 180 degreet stat, it would run over 220 constantly. I was sure it was a head/gasket problem. Then the water-pump crapped out on me(12/20/00). I replaced it, it didn't help the cooling situation any, but eliminated one possibilty. Then I decided to replace the radiator even though it had no apparent problems(3/10/01???). I bought a stock replacement from AutoZone, and that fixed the problem. My Formula never hits over half-way to 220 unless I am in a very slow line(10 minutes+). Now with Rodneys new lower temp fan switch(I went with 210/195???) I can completely throw out my manual fan switch on the dash, with no worries, and I never need to keep my eye on the temp gauge to know when to flip the switch. <P>My '86SE(106K miles) runs hot(ter) so I plan to replace the radiator in it too. Fiero especially seems susceptible to clogged radiator over time, doesn't seem to be very miles related. <P>I would suggest to anyone with a warmer running engine to try a new radiator. Only about $60, and an hour of time. Put in a new water-pump, 180 degree stat for summer, and lower temp fan switch, and my coolant temps rarely ever change. Plus, with this sensor, I can still run a 195 stat for winter(180 never seems to work in colder weather below 50*).<P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pffsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||maryjane||08-05-2001||09:26 PM||||That sounds good. Would you think a rad change would be necessary even if the old flow tested ok? Can a rad flow ok & still have enough scale to inhibit heat disapation?||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||08-05-2001||09:44 PM||||I don't know much about flow rates/scale etc on the Fiero, so someone else with more info will have to answer that. I never had my old radiator tested, I figured it would cost nearly as much to have the original reconditioned as it would to buy a new one. So I have no idea what it was flowing or how plugged up it was, but it must have been pretty bad even though the coolant was always clean of "floaties."<P>My new radiator was made by "readi-rad"(or similar spelling), had the a/t cooling lines for auto cars(mines a 5-speed) and was painted gloss black everywhere, very shiny and pretty. It bolted in just like the original too. I looked, and I replaced it in early April. 4 monts so far, and no signs of any problems.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||08-05-2001||10:19 PM||||The driver's side tank started leaking on my GT, so I pulled the tanks from a 4 cyl radiator to put on mine. When I got the tank off, I was amazed at how much crap was in there! Over half the radiator would not flow any water due to sludge, old silicone and gasket material. It took a long time to clear all the passages, but now the temp runs in the lower side of "normal". ||||reg||1
Z||000004||ka4nkf||08-05-2001||10:27 PM||||I have the 210/200 from Rodney and it is great best thing I ever did to my V6 Fiero.My temp never gets to 220 and I still have the 195 Stat. I put a blue light below the trunk button and this tells me when the  fan is on. It never comes on during highway driving||||reg||1
Z||000005||AusFiero||08-07-2001||05:38 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>That sounds good. Would you think a rad change would be necessary even if the old flow tested ok? Can a rad flow ok & still have enough scale to inhibit heat disapation?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mine flow tested ok but was 60% blocked when I had it reconditioned. Cam anyone confirm this new $60 radiator deal. I find that very hard to believe considering we usually triple the prices in Australia to get a Fiero part. I know a new one here for a Fiero is $600, that would make it about US$200. But I suppose I only ever buy genuine GM parts and there may be cheaper. But from experience, you get what you pay for in this world. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||08-07-2001||06:45 PM||||It was right around $60, add tax and crap and it was probably right around $65.<P>It wasn't a factory GM unit from the dealer.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-07-2001||08:05 PM||||The number one killer of cooling systems is lack of coolant changes. The stuff only lasts 2-3 years.<P>Every other cause is way behind.<P>Avoid using Tap water and Never use "softened" water. These often contain minerals chlorine or worse Salt. Use distilled or deionized water. Water from a "reverse osmosis" filter will also work.<P>Silicone waste gets there only one way... incompetant repair work. Even most "pro" repair people use way to much of that crap.<P>Anytime you work on the system.... remember to clean out the recovery tank. This thing is resposible for allot of road dirt finding it's way into the system. If posible stick a piece of "scotch brite" or something in large holes to filter the air a bit. Make sure the filter can't find a way to plug the line to the radiator. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||1
